1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. Qe2 Be7 6. O-O d6 7. c3 O-O 8. Rd1
Bg4 9. d4 Nd7 10. h3 Bxf3 11. Qxf3 Bf6 12. d5 Ne7 13. Nd2 Ng6 14. Nf1 Nb6 15.
Bb3 Bg5 16. g3 Bxc1 17. Raxc1 Qd7 18. Kh2 Ne7 19. Ne3 Kh8 20. Rf1 Ng8 21. c4
Nc8 22. Qe2 Nce7 23. f4 f6 24. f5 Nh6 25. g4 Nf7 26. Bc2 Ng8 27. h4 Qe7 28. Rg1
h6 29. Nf1 Rfb8 30. Bd3 Qe8 31. b3 b5 32. Nd2 bxc4 33. Bxc4 {This recapture is
illegible as regards the capturing piece but, after a lot of thought, I have
concluded it must be the bishop that makes this capture for the subsequent
logic to work.} Qd8 34. Rg2 ({This is not what the score gives, but the score
simply doesn't work, whereas this does, perfectly, as regards the details of
the denouement. The score has 34.P-R4 R-Kt3 35.Kt-B3 Q-K2 36.R(B2)-Kt2 but
there is no way to achieve the finale convincingly with the pieces in that
configuration as the Q has to be where it is for the kingside attack to work,
and the rooks cannot by-pass the queen on the 2nd rank as the score would
suggest.} 34. Rc2 Rb6 35. Nf3 Qe7 36. Rb2 Rab8 37. g5 fxg5 38. hxg5 hxg5 39.
Nxg5 Nfh6 40. Qh5 Nf6 41. Qh4 Nh7 42. Qh3 Nxg5 43. Rxg5 Rg8) 34... Rb6 35. Nf3
Qe7 36. Rcg1 Rab8 37. g5 fxg5 38. hxg5 hxg5 39. Nxg5 Nfh6 40. Qh5 Nf6 41. Qh4
Nh7 42. Qh3 Nxg5 43. Rxg5 Rg8 44. Rh5 Kh7 45. Rg6 Qf8 46. Qd3 Qc8 47. Qe3 Qf8
48. Qg5 Rb8 49. f6 a5 50. f7 Rh8 51. Qf5 {Source: Glasgow Herald, 26 Sept 1925}
